    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/jobs/cultural-collections-officer-tyler-collection-hobart-tas-australia": {
      "title": "Cultural Collections Officer (Tyler Collection)",
      "description": "Cultural Collections Officer (Tyler Collection) - Hobart, TAS, Australia Skip to main content Visit UTAS My Profile Kiosk mode Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Video Player is loading. Play Video Play Skip Backward Skip Forward Mute Current Time 0:00 / Duration 0:00 Loaded : 0% Stream Type LIVE Seek to live, currently behind live LIVE Remaining Time - 0:00 1x Playback Rate Chapters Chapters Descriptions descriptions off , selected Subtitles captions and subtitles off , selected Audio Track Picture-in-Picture Fullscreen This is a modal window. Beginning of dialog window. Escape will cancel and close the window. Text Color White Black Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Opaque Semi-Transparent Text Background Color Black White Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Opaque Semi-Transparent Transparent Caption Area Background Color Black White Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Transparent Semi-Transparent Opaque Font Size 50% 75% 100% 125% 150% 175% 200% 300% 400% Text Edge Style None Raised Depressed Uniform Drop shadow Font Family Proportional Sans-Serif Monospace Sans-Serif Proportional Serif Monospace Serif Casual Script Small Caps Reset Done Close Modal Dialog End of dialog window. Cultural Collections Officer (Tyler Collection) 503502 Hobart, TAS Part time Closing on: 16 Nov 2026 Ensure ethical, professional and culturally informed collections management practices Enable meaningful research, teaching and community engagement through collection access and interpretation Ongoing, Part Time (0.5) position based in Hobart The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. About the opportunity The Cultural Collections Officer plays a key role in the care, presentation and accessibility of the University’s cultural collections, with a particular focus on the Tyler Collection of Romanian and Modern Art. Working under the general direction of the Manager, Curatorial & Collections, the position coordinates the day-to-day preparation, documentation, display and movement of collection items, ensuring they are managed and presented in accordance with professional standards. The role also maintains collection records and supports exhibition readiness across University sites, including the ongoing operation of the dedicated Tyler Collection exhibition space within the Carington Smith Library. The position contributes to provenance research, improves collection records, and prepares draft interpretive materials that support teaching, learning and public engagement. It also coordinates the operational delivery of established Tyler Collection research and engagement programs, providing support for visiting researchers and artists and facilitating access to collection resources. Working within contemporary collections practice, the role recognises and responds to provenance, cultural rights and repatriation considerations, including moral rights, ICIP and artist rights, while ensuring donor obligations and collection-specific requirements are upheld. Complex or sensitive matters relating to governance, donor relationships or collection display are referred appropriately. What you’ll do: Manage collection stewardship activities, including object handling, condition reporting, loans, accessioning, display preparation and risk management, in accordance with professional standards. Coordinate the day-to-day delivery of Tyler Collection programs and support researchers, artists and visitors through effective access, logistics and exhibition space management. Maintain accurate collection records in TMS and contribute to data quality and records integrity. Undertake provenance research and prepare draft interpretive content, identifying and escalating ethical, cultural and rights-related issues where required. Provide practical curatorial support on object selection, display, handling and interpretation. Support teaching, learning and research activities through collection access, object preparation and collection tours. Apply WHS and University requirements, escalating non-routine risks and sensitive matters as appropriate. Contribute positively to team projects and operations, supporting broader cultural collections activities as required. ­ What we’re looking for: A degree level qualification in a relevant area (Museum Studies, Heritage, Curatorial Studies, History or similar discipline) or an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education or education/training. Demonstrated experience in artwork handling, preparation and installation, ensuring objects are displayed, stored and transported safely and appropriately. Demonstrated experience managing collections documentation and records, including metadata, image libraries, location control, condition reporting and collections management systems (e.g. EMu, TMS or Vernon). Experience supporting researchers, fellows and other collection users through effective coordination of access, scheduling and material preparation. Ability to conduct provenance research and prepare interpretive materials, with an understanding of decolonial collections practice and cultural, legal and ethical rights considerations. Demonstrated ability to contribute to curatorial projects through advice on object selection, display outcomes, handling requirements and ethical considerations. Highly organised, with proven ability to coordinate multiple tasks and priorities while meeting agreed timelines and service expectations. Strong communication and stakeholder management skills, with sound judgement and discretion when dealing with sensitive collection, exhibition or donor-related matters specific to the Tyler Collection. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 5 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $105,665 (pro rata) comprising base salary within the range of $80,869 to $90,312 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Katrina Ross – Manager, Curatorial and Collections, at k.ross@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 503502 - Cultural Collections Officer (Tyler Collection).pdf Applications close Monday, 16 November 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) c38849a5 Last Name (required) d4a09b39 Email (required) 7e183786 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs AFWI Research Contracts Manager Hobart, TAS. Launceston, TAS. Statewide (all locations in Tasmania), TAS Laboratory & Research Support, Legal & Compliance, Management Support nationally significant research by leading contract management and governance across AFWI's research portfolio. Senior Lecturer Psychology Hobart, TAS. Statewide (all locations in Tasmania), TAS Teaching Intensive Shape the future of psychology education and clinical training in Tasmania. Join the University of Tasmania's School of Psychological Sciences and contribute to high-quality teaching, supervision, research and professional engagement that supports the next generation of psychologists. Technical Officer Launceston, TAS Infrastructure & Property, Teaching Support Firefighter, defence, maintenance or trade background? Put your practical skills to work at the Australian Maritime College's Emergency Response Centre and help train the next generation of maritime professionals Sign up for job alerts Job Alert bea0f2dd Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Library Library Employment Type Part time Part time Locations Hobart, TAS, Australia Hobart, TAS, Australia First Name (required) ad1c4f40 Last Name (required) b8290360 Email (required) 05019f70 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you © 2026  University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Cookie Preferences Copyright Statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
